+def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:
+    """
+    Emit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks, let them ricochet off the grid boundary, and recolor the source frame only when the bounced path returns to it.
+
+    Input structure:
+    - The most common color is the background, and each monochrome 2x2 block is wrapped by a 4x4 frame of one shared wall color.
+    - Frame border gaps are background cells. In this fused variant, generation uses corner gaps, so each gap launches a diagonal beam outward from its block.
+
+    Rule:
+    - Every gap seeds a beam in the inner block's color. The beam keeps moving diagonally, reflecting off any grid edge by mirroring the blocked axis instead of terminating there.
+    - Away from frames, beam interactions still follow the original collision grammar: 90-degree meetings leave a blank pivot and turn toward the bisector, while 45-degree meetings merge into the alternating zigzag path.
+    - A frame is no longer recolored at launch. Instead, once a reflected beam later lands on one of its own frame's gap cells, that gap's incident frame side(s) recolor into the beam color, so repeated returns from different gaps can accumulate on the same frame.
+
+    Key insight:
+    - The important dependency is temporal: you must trace the bounced trajectory first, then apply frame recolor only at the moment the beam comes back to its own opening. The frame edit is therefore evidence of the reflected path, not a separate local decoration.
+    """
+    return _solve(grid, collect_stats=False)
